.TH LSTORE 1 "2026-05" "BusyPipe 1.0" "BusyPipe Manual"
.SH NAME
lstore \- file-based key-value store for BusyPipe
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B lstore \-\-db \fIPATH\fR \-\-put \-\-key\-field \fIFIELD\fR [\-\-ttl \fISEC\fR] [\-\-stats]
.B lstore \-\-db \fIPATH\fR \-\-get    \fIKEY\fR
.B lstore \-\-db \fIPATH\fR \-\-delete \fIKEY\fR
.B lstore \-\-db \fIPATH\fR \-\-list
.B lstore \-\-db \fIPATH\fR \-\-cleanup [\-\-stats]
.B lstore \-\-db \fIPATH\fR \-\-count
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lstore
provides persistent, TTL-aware, file-backed key-value storage for the
BusyPipe pipeline.  The database is a plain-text TSV file — readable with
any standard tool — making it suitable for embedded and container environments.

It is the third stage of the BusyPipe ETL pipeline:

.nf
  lparser | lfilter | lstore
.fi

.SS Storage format
Each line of the database file has the form:

.nf
  key<TAB>expires_at_epoch<TAB>raw_csv_row
.fi

An \fIexpires_at_epoch\fR of \fB0\fR means the record never expires.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-db " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to the database file (created automatically on first write).
Required for all modes.
.TP
.BR \-\-put
Read CSV rows from stdin and append them to the database.
Requires \fI\-\-key\-field\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-key\-field " " \fIFIELD\fR
Name of the CSV column whose value becomes the record key.
.TP
.BR \-\-ttl " " \fISEC\fR
Time-to-live in seconds.  Omit or set to \fB0\fR for no expiry.
.TP
.BR \-\-get " " \fIKEY\fR
Print the most-recently written, non-expired record for \fIKEY\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-delete " " \fIKEY\fR
Remove all records with the given key (rewrites the database file).
.TP
.BR \-\-list
Print all non-expired records: one \fIkey<TAB>value\fR per line.
.TP
.BR \-\-cleanup
Rewrite the database file, discarding all expired records.
.TP
.BR \-\-count
Print the number of non-expired records.
.TP
.BR \-\-stats
Print operation statistics to stderr (written, skipped, removed, etc.).
.TP
.BR \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, missing key-field, or I/O failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Write error log entries with 1-hour TTL
.nf
  lparser \-\-format nginx \-\-csv < access.log \\
    | lfilter \-\-where 'status>=400' \\
    | lstore  \-\-db errors.tsv \-\-put \-\-key\-field ip \-\-ttl 3600
.fi
.SS Query a specific key
.nf
  lstore \-\-db errors.tsv \-\-get 192.168.0.4
.fi
.SS List all valid entries
.nf
  lstore \-\-db errors.tsv \-\-list
.fi
.SS Clean up expired entries and show stats
.nf
  lstore \-\-db errors.tsv \-\-cleanup \-\-stats
.fi
.SS Count remaining entries
.nf
  lstore \-\-db errors.tsv \-\-count
.fi
.SH IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.B Write buffering:
\fB\-\-put\fR buffers output and flushes every 64 lines or 128 KiB to reduce
syscall overhead in high-throughput pipelines.
.PP
.B Atomic rewrite:
\fB\-\-cleanup\fR and \fB\-\-delete\fR write to a temporary file and rename it over the
original, preventing data loss on crash.
.PP
.B Append semantics:
Multiple records with the same key may exist; \fB\-\-get\fR returns the last
(most-recently written) valid record.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The TSV storage format can be processed by standard GNU tools:
.nf
  awk -F'\\t' '{print $1}' errors.tsv   # list all keys
  cut -f3 errors.tsv                   # raw CSV values
.fi
.SH BUGS
Concurrent writers are not safe (no file locking).
Records are not deduplicated on write; use \fB\-\-cleanup\fR periodically.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lparser (1),
.BR lfilter (1),
.BR awk (1),
.BR cut (1)
